course: dictionary
lesson: 105
title: Adjectives: describing words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An adjective is a describing word. It tells us more about a noun, which is a person, place or thing. In «the tall tree», the word tall tells us what kind of tree it is.
explain: Adjectives can come before a noun, as in «a tall tree», or after verbs like «is» and «seems», as in «the tree is tall». Both are adjectives, because both tell us more about the tree.
explain: Some adjectives end in -ly, such as «friendly» and «lonely». Check what the word describes: in «a friendly dog», friendly describes the dog, so it is an adjective, even though it ends in -ly.
fact: An adjective describes a noun: a person, place or thing.
fact: Adjectives can come before a noun, as in «a tall tree», or after is and seems, as in «the tree is tall».
fact: Words like «big», «red», «happy» and «three» are all adjectives.
fact: Adjectives usually go in this order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose, as in «a lovely little old round red French wooden toy box».
fact: Comparatives and superlatives change adjectives: big, bigger, biggest; good, better, best.
exercise: In «a lovely little old round red French wooden toy box», which word tells us the colour? | box | toy | *red | lovely
exercise: Which word is the adjective in «The tree is tall»? | is | The | tree | *tall
exercise: «A friendly dog ran to the gate.» Why is friendly an adjective? | It ends in -ly, so it must be an adverb | It tells us how the dog ran | *It describes the noun dog | It is a verb
test: What is the superlative of good? | gooder | goodest | bigger | *best
test: In «a tiny green frog», which word tells us the size? | green | a | frog | *tiny
exam: In «the lonely cat sat by the door», what does the adjective lonely describe? | sat | the door | by the | *the cat
